 # Volkmann (Rake) Retractor
 
-**Single-ended handheld rake retractor with multiple sharp or blunt prongs in a row** at the end of a flat handle — the **multi-prong workhorse** for retracting skin, subcutaneous tissue, muscle, and periosteum in deeper or wider fields than a [Senn](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/retractors/senn) can manage. Named for **Richard von Volkmann (1830–1889)**, one of the most influential surgeons of the 19th century, founding figure of orthopedic surgery, early adopter of Lister's antiseptic technique in Germany, and namesake of **Volkmann's ischemic contracture** and the **Hueter-Volkmann law**.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)</sup>
+The **Volkmann**, also cataloged as **Volkman**, is a handheld single-ended hook or rake retractor. Current product families include one- through six-prong tips and sharp or blunt variants; dimensions and handle geometry vary substantially by model.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)[[2]](#ref2)</sup>
 
-## Design
+## Current Designs
 
-- **Working end**: **multiple prongs (tines) in a single row** on a transverse bar — the "rake."
-- **Prong-count variants**: **1, 2, 3, 4, or 6 prongs**. **2-, 3-, and 4-prong** are the daily workhorses; **6-prong** for the broadest exposure.
-- **Sharp vs blunt prongs**: sharp for firm grip on skin / dense subcutaneous tissue / periosteum; **blunt** for muscle / fascia and near delicate structures.
-- **Prongs curved slightly inward** — hook configuration prevents slip-out under traction.
-- **Handle**: flat straight stainless-steel strip, ~ **20–22 cm (8–8.5 in)** overall.
-- **Single-ended** (no smooth blade on the other end — that distinguishes Volkmann from [Senn](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/retractors/senn)).
-- **Material**: surgical-grade stainless steel, autoclavable.
-- **No locking** — handheld.
+The name identifies a family rather than one universal specification:
 
-## Reconstructive-Urology and Urogyn Uses
+- A current Sklar single-hook model is straight, sharp, 8 inches (203 mm), reusable, stainless steel and supplied nonsterile. Sklar states that its wider family includes one to six prongs in sharp or blunt configurations.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)</sup>
+- Current Sklar instrument sets list 8.5-inch two-, three-, four- and six-prong models, including both sharp and blunt versions.<sup>[[2]](#ref2)</sup>
+- A current Teleflex/Pilling six-prong sharp model is only 4.5 inches (11.5 cm) long with a 2 cm-wide blade.<sup>[[3]](#ref3)</sup>
 
-The Volkmann rake fills the **wider-than-Senn / shallower-than-Richardson** niche on RU/urogyn trays — particularly useful when the field is broader than a single Senn can hold and a Richardson would be overkill:
+Accordingly, the prior idea that all Volkmann retractors are approximately 20–22 cm with a flat strip handle is incorrect. Verify the selected model's prong count, tip, length, handle, supply state and reprocessing instructions.
 
-- **Open inguinal incisions** — orchidopexy, hernia repair, groin-flap harvest (Singapore / pudendal-thigh), inguinal LND for penile-cancer staging.
-- **Open scrotal incisions** for IPP / AUS / hydrocelectomy / varicocelectomy when the field is wider than the Senn-Miller can hold — particularly large hydroceles, scrotal-skin flap-reconstruction cases, large-scrotal-mass excisions.
-- **Vulvar / introital reconstruction** — broader retraction during labiaplasty, vestibulectomy, post-defibulation closure, vulvar-cancer-adjunct exposure (when scoped to reconstructive consequence).
-- **Open pubectomy and pubic-ramus exposure** during PFUI repair — periosteal retraction at the inferior pubic ramus, paired with [Langenbeck](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/retractors/langenbeck).
-- **Open suprapubic incisions** — at the skin / subcutaneous layer for SPC placement, AUS reservoir, small open BNR.
-- **Perineal exposure** during posterior urethroplasty before the [Perineal Bookwalter (Jordan)](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/retractors/perineal-bookwalter) or [Turner-Warwick](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/retractors/turner-warwick) ring takes over — handheld initial retraction across the perineum.
-- **Closure phase** of any open RU case — the rake redeploys for the subcutaneous / skin closure layer.
+## Practical Uses
 
-## Volkmann vs Senn vs Skin Hook
+A Volkmann rake can provide handheld traction on skin, subcutaneous tissue, fascia or other exposed tissue when its prong shape and size suit the layer. In reconstructive urology and pelvic surgery, practical examples include superficial portions of inguinal, scrotal, perineal, vulvar or suprapubic incisions and selected periosteal exposure.
 
-| Feature | **Volkmann** | [**Senn (Senn-Miller)**](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/retractors/senn) | **Skin hook** |
-|---|---|---|---|
-| **Ends** | Single (rake only) | Double (rake + smooth blade) | Single (hook only) |
-| **Prongs** | 1, 2, 3, 4, or 6 | Fixed 3 | Single or double |
-| **Length** | ~ 20–22 cm | ~ 16 cm | ~ 16 cm |
-| **Field width** | Wider | Smaller | Smallest, focal |
-| **Tissue grip** | Distributed across prongs | Distributed across 3 prongs | Single-point focal |
-| **Best fit** | Wider / deeper skin and superficial layers | Small-field skin / subcutaneous | Fine flap edges, delicate skin |
+These examples are technique context, not evidence that a Volkmann is required or superior for any named operation. Sharp tips can puncture or tear tissue and present a sharps hazard; blunt tips can still cause pressure injury. Keep tips visible when feasible and choose the least traumatic configuration that provides adequate exposure.
 
-The Volkmann is **larger and more robust** than the Senn; the Volkmann's prong-count flexibility lets the surgeon match the rake to the wound width.
+## Volkmann, Senn and Skin Hooks
 
-## Comparison to Adjacent Handheld Retractors
+| Instrument family | Typical architecture | Practical distinction |
+|---|---|---|
+| **Volkmann/Volkman** | Single-ended hook or multi-prong rake | Model-dependent width and depth; handheld |
+| [**Senn / Senn-Miller**](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/retractors/senn) | Double-ended rake and blade | Compact superficial exposure; exact tip varies |
+| [**Skin hook**](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/retractors/skin-hooks) | One or two fine hooks in common models | Focal skin-edge traction |
+| [**Weitlaner**](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/retractors/weitlaner) | Opposing pronged arms with ratchet | Self-retaining bilateral exposure |
 
-| Retractor | Tissue grip | Depth | Best fit |
-|---|---|---|---|
-| **Volkmann (rake)** | Excellent (prongs engage tissue) | Superficial-to-intermediate | Skin / muscle / periosteum, wider fields |
-| [**Senn (Senn-Miller)**](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/retractors/senn) | Excellent (rake) / moderate (blade) | Superficial-to-intermediate | Smaller skin / superficial-muscle fields |
-| [**Army-Navy**](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/retractors/army-navy) | Moderate (smooth) | Skin / subcutaneous | General superficial wound retraction |
-| [**Richardson**](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/retractors/richardson) | Moderate (shelf-blade) | Fascia / muscle | Abdominal-wall layered retraction |
-| [**Deaver**](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/retractors/deaver) | Limited (smooth) | Deep cavity | Deep abdominal / pelvic |
+Prong count alone does not establish how force is distributed in living tissue or whether one instrument reduces tearing, fatigue or wound complications. No reviewed comparative study supports such outcome claims.
 
-## Mechanism
+## Use Principles
 
-1. **Prong engagement**: prongs placed into the wound edge — sharp penetrate for grip; blunt compress and hook.
-2. **Lateral retraction**: assistant pulls handle laterally to displace tissue; multiple prongs distribute force across a wider area than a single skin hook, reducing tearing.
-3. **Sustained exposure**: flat handle allows steady prolonged retraction with minimal fatigue.
-4. **Adjustable depth and angle** by varying prong-insertion depth and traction angle.
+1. Match tip sharpness, prong count and size to the exposed layer and wound geometry.
+2. Place only as deeply as necessary and confirm what lies beneath sharp tips.
+3. Apply the minimum traction needed for exposure; reassess tissue color, tension and tip position during sustained use.
+4. Reposition deliberately rather than repeatedly puncturing the same wound edge.
+5. Transition to a deeper or self-retaining system when the operative target exceeds the model's safe reach.
 
-## Limitations
+No evidence reviewed here establishes a universal insertion angle, a fixed prong-to-wound-width algorithm or a procedure-specific release interval.
 
-- **Handheld** — requires an assistant; fatigues; switch to self-retaining ([Weitlaner](#), [Adson-Beckman](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/retractors/adson-beckman), [Lone Star](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/retractors/lone-star)) for prolonged retraction.
-- **Sharp-prong tissue trauma** — puncture, wound-edge ischemia, glove perforation, sharps-injury risk to surgical team. Blunt-prong variant mitigates.
-- **Limited depth** — superficial-to-intermediate only; switch to Richardson / Deaver for deeper layers.
-- **Single-ended** — no smooth-blade alternative on the same instrument (vs Senn).
-- **Not for delicate / fine flap edges** — single skin hook is preferable for facial / glanular / fine labial work.
+## Historical Context
 
-## Technique
+Richard von Volkmann (1830–1889) was a prominent German surgeon, early German adopter of Listerian antisepsis and the namesake of Volkmann ischemic contracture and the Hueter-Volkmann law.<sup>[[4]](#ref4)</sup> The reviewed historical article supports his biography but does not document the design provenance of every modern rake pattern sold under his name.
 
-1. **Match prong number to wound width**: **2-prong** for narrow approaches (small orthopedic / pediatric inguinal); **3- or 4-prong** for standard incisions; **6-prong** for the widest exposures.
-2. **Sharp vs blunt**: sharp for skin / dense subcutaneous / periosteum; blunt for muscle / fascia and near nerves / vessels / ureter / dorsal NVB.
-3. **Angle**: ~ **45–60°** from tissue surface — too perpendicular pulls prongs out; too shallow loses effective depth.
-4. **Minimize force**: steady moderate traction; excessive force tears tissue and bends prongs.
-5. **Minimize repositioning**: each removal-and-reinsertion produces additional trauma — plan placement carefully.
-6. **Protect underlying structures**: when using sharp prongs near nerve / vessel / ureter, ensure tips are directed away; otherwise switch to blunt.
-7. **Transition to deeper retractors** as the dissection progresses (rake → Richardson → Deaver / Bookwalter).
